    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d15">

                <pb facs="0079" n="44" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v06/pg44"/>

                <head>15. Letter From President Carter to
                    Soviet General Secretary Brezhnev<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d15-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1977-03-24">March 24, 1977</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <opener>
                    <salute>To General Secretary Brezhnev
                    </salute>
                </opener>
                <p>The substantive answer to your recent letter<anchor n="2" xml:id="d15-f2"/> will
                    be presented in person by Secretary Vance. However, I wish to express my hope that his visit to
                    Moscow will result in substantial general agreements regarding arms reduction
                    and the enhancement of cooperation between our two countries as we search
                    together for a more peaceful and friendly world.</p>
                <p>I hope to continue our correspondence after Mr. Vance’s return, and look forward to an early meeting with you.
                    The Secretary of State has my complete confidence, he and I have spent hours
                    together preparing for this important trip, and he is qualified to speak
                    authoritatively on my behalf.</p>
                <p>You have my personal good wishes.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Jimmy
                            Carter</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d15-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d15-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Staff Material, Office,
                    Outside the System File, Box 69, Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR): Brezhnev–Carter
                    Correspondence: 3–5/77. No classification marking. The letter is handwritten. In
                    a March 24 covering memorandum to Carter, Brzezinski
                    commented, “It seems to Cy [Vance] and
                    to me that it might be a nice gesture if you were to write a brief (perhaps
                    hand-written) personal note to Brezhnev.” Brzezinski also
                    provided three points for Carter to
                    consider including, all of which are in Carter’s hand-written letter.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d15-f2" n="2" xml:id="d15-f2-content">
